== 5. God as All Mercy

The fifth device that Satan has to draw the soul to sin is to present God to the soul as one made up all of mercy. “Oh,” says Satan, “you need not make such a matter of sin. You need not be so fearful of sin, nor so unwilling to sin. For God is a God of mercy, a God full of mercy, a God who delights in mercy, a God who is ready to show mercy, a God who is never weary of showing mercy, a God more inclined to pardon his people than to punish his people. And therefore he will not seize on the soul’s sins to punish it. Why then,” says Satan, “should you make such a matter of sin?”

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are these.]

The first remedy is seriously to consider that it is the sorest judgment in this world to be left to sin on any pretense whatever. Oh, unhappy man, when God leaves you to yourself#footnote[#emph[Humanum est peccare, Diabolicum perseverare, & Angelicum resurgere] (Augustine), that is, it is a human thing to fall into sin, a devilish thing to persevere in it, and an angelic or supernatural thing to rise from it.] and does not resist you in your sins! Woe, woe to him at whose sins God winks! When God lets the way to hell be a smooth and pleasant way, that is hell on this side of hell, and a dreadful sign of God’s indignation against a man, a token of his rejection, and a sign that God does not intend good to him. That is a sad word: “Ephraim is joined to idols; leave him alone!” #emph[He will not be counseled or corrected; he has made a match with wickedness; he shall have his bellyful of it; he falls with his eyes open; let him fall at his own peril.] And that is a terrible saying: “So I gave them up to the lusts of their own hearts,#footnote[Ps 81:12. Hos 4:17.] to follow their own counsels.” A soul given up to sin is a soul ripe for hell, a soul hurrying to destruction. Ah,#footnote[#emph[A me, me salva Domine.] Deliver me, #emph[O Lord,] from that evil man, myself. Augustine.] Lord! This mercy I humbly beg: that whatever you give me up to, you will not give me up to the ways of my own heart. If you will give me up to be afflicted, or tempted, or reproached, etc., I will patiently sit down and say, “It is the Lord; let him do with me what seems good in his own eyes.” Do anything with me, lay what burden you will upon me, so long as you do not give me up to the ways of my own heart, etc.

The second remedy against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider that God is as just as he is merciful. As the Scriptures speak of him as a very merciful God, so they speak of him#footnote[2 Pet 2:4, 5, 6. God hanged them up on gibbets, as it were, so that others might hear, and fear, and do no more so wickedly.] as a very just God. Witness his casting the angels out of heaven and binding them in chains of darkness until the judgment of the great day. Witness his turning Adam out of paradise, his drowning of the old world, and his raining hell out of heaven upon Sodom. Witness all the crosses, losses, sicknesses, and diseases that are in the world. Witness Tophet, which was prepared of old; witness his “storing up wrath for the day of wrath, when God’s righteous judgment will be revealed.” But above all, witness the pouring out of all his wrath upon his bosom Son, when he bore the sins of his people and cried out, “My God, My God, why have You forsaken Me?”#footnote[Matt 27:46.]

The third remedy against this device of Satan is seriously to consider that sins against mercy will bring the greatest and the sorest judgments upon men’s heads and hearts. Mercy is #emph[Alpha;] justice is #emph[Omega.] David, speaking of these attributes, places mercy in the vanguard and justice in the rearguard, saying, “I will sing of Your mercy and justice.”#footnote[Ps 101:1.] When mercy is despised, then justice takes the throne. God is like a#footnote[#emph[Quantò gradus altior tantò casus gravior,] the higher we are in dignity, the more grievous is our fall and misery.] prince who does not send his army against rebels before he has sent his pardon and proclaimed it by a herald. He first hangs out the white flag of mercy. If this wins men in, they are happy forever; but if they hold out, then God will put out his red flag of justice and judgment. If the one is despised, the other will be felt with a vengeance. See this in the Israelites.#footnote[#emph[Deus tardus est ad iram sed tarditatem gravitate poenae compensat.] God is slow to anger, but he makes up for his slowness with the severity of the punishment.] He loved them and chose them when they were in their blood and most unlovely. He multiplied them, not by means but by miracle; for from seventy souls they grew in a few years to six hundred thousand. The more they were oppressed, the more they prospered: like chamomile, the more you tread it, the more you spread it; or like a palm tree, the more it is pressed down, the further it spreads; or like fire, the more it is raked, the more it burns. Their mercies came in upon them like Job’s messengers, one on the heels of another.#footnote[If we abuse mercy to serve our lusts, then, in Salvian’s phrase, God will rain hell out of heaven rather than not punish such sins.] “He took off their sackcloth and clothed them with gladness, and surrounded them with songs of deliverance; he carried them on eagles’ wings; he kept them as the apple of his eye,” etc. But they, abusing his mercy, became the greatest objects of his wrath. As I know of no man who can reckon up their mercies, so I know of no man who can sum up the miseries that have come upon them for their sins. For as our Savior prophesied concerning Jerusalem, “that not one stone#footnote[Vespasian broke into their city at Kidron, where they took Christ, on the same feast day that Christ was taken. He whipped them where they whipped Christ; he sold twenty Jews for a penny, as they sold Christ for 30 pence. #emph[Andr. cat.]] would be left on another,” so it was fulfilled forty years after his ascension by Vespasian the emperor and his son Titus. They besieged Jerusalem, and the Jews were oppressed with a grievous famine, in which their food was old shoes, old leather, old hay, and the dung of beasts. There died, partly by the sword and partly by the famine, eleven hundred thousand of the poorer sort. Two thousand in one night were disemboweled; six thousand were burned in a porch of the temple; the whole city was sacked and burned and leveled to the ground; and ninety-seven thousand were taken captive and put to base and miserable service, as Eusebius and Josephus say. And to this day, in all parts of the world, are they not the refuse of the world? None are less loved, and none more abhorred, than they. And so Capernaum, which was lifted up to heaven, was threatened to be#footnote[Men are therefore the worse because they ought to be better, and shall be deeper in hell because heaven was offered to them, but they refused it. #emph[Ingentia beneficia flagitia, supplicia,] good turns add weight to unkindnesses, and men’s offenses are increased by their obligation. ἀμελήσαντες: shift off, disregard.] thrown down to hell. No souls fall so low into hell (if they fall) as those souls who by a hand of mercy are lifted up nearest to heaven. You#super[+] careless souls who are so apt to abuse mercy, consider this: in the days of the gospel, the plagues that God inflicts on the despisers and abusers of mercy are usually spiritual plagues, such as blindness of mind, hardness of heart, and numbness of conscience. These are ten thousand times worse than the worst of outward plagues that can befall you#super[+]. And therefore, though you#super[+] may escape temporal judgments, yet you#super[+] will not escape spiritual judgments: “How shall we escape if we neglect such a great salvation?” says the apostle. Oh, therefore, whenever Satan presents God to the soul as one made up all of mercy, so that he may draw you to do wickedly, tell him that sins against mercy will bring the greatest misery upon the soul, and that therefore, whatever becomes of you, you will not sin against mercy, etc.

The fourth remedy against this device of Satan is seriously to consider that though God’s general mercy is over all his works, yet his special mercy#footnote[Augustus at his solemn feasts gave trifles to some, but gold to others on whom his heart was most set. So God by a hand of general mercy gives these poor trifles, outward blessings, to those whom he least loves; but his gold, his special mercy, is only toward those on whom his heart is most set.] is confined to those who are divinely qualified. So in Exodus 34:6, 7: “And the #smallcaps[Lord] passed in front of him and proclaimed: ‘The #smallcaps[Lord], the #smallcaps[Lord] God, merciful and gracious, slow to anger, abounding in goodness and faithfulness, maintaining mercy to thousands, forgiving iniquity, transgression, and sin. Yet He will by no means leave the guilty unpunished.’” Exod 20:6: “and showing mercy to thousands of those who love Me and keep My commandments.” Ps 25:10: “All the #smallcaps[Lord]’s ways are mercy and faithfulness to those who keep His covenant and His decrees.” Ps 32:10: “Many are the sorrows of the wicked, but mercy surrounds him who trusts in the #smallcaps[Lord].” Ps 33:18: “Surely the eyes of the #smallcaps[Lord] are on those who fear Him, on those whose hope is in His mercy.” Ps 103:11: “For as high as the heavens are above the earth, so great is His mercy toward those who fear Him.” Verse 17: “But from everlasting to everlasting the mercy of the #smallcaps[Lord] is upon those who fear Him.” When Satan attempts to draw you to sin by presenting God as a God all made up of mercy, oh, then reply that though God’s general mercy extends to all the works of his hand, yet his special mercy is confined to those who are divinely qualified: to those who love him and keep his commandments, to those who trust in him, who by hope hang upon him, and who fear him. Reply that you must be such a one here, or else you can never be happy hereafter; you must partake of his special mercy, or else perish eternally in everlasting misery, notwithstanding God’s general mercy.

The fifth remedy against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider that those who were once glorious on earth, and are now triumphing in heaven, looked upon the mercy of God as the most powerful reason to preserve them from sin and to fence their souls against sin, and not as an encouragement#footnote[Ps 26:3, 4, 5, 6.] to sin. Ps 26:3, 4, 5, 6: “For Your loving kindness is before my eyes, and I have walked in Your truth. I do not sit with deceitful men, nor keep company with hypocrites. I hate the mob of evildoers, and refuse to sit with the wicked.” So Joseph strengthens himself against sin by remembering mercy. “How then could I,” he says, “do such a great#footnote[Gen 39:9.] evil and sin against God?” He had fixed his eye on mercy, and therefore sin could not enter, though the irons entered into his soul. His soul, being taken with mercy, was not moved by the shamelessness of his master’s wife. Satan knocked often at the door, but the sight of mercy would not allow Joseph to answer or open. Joseph, like a pearl in a puddle, keeps his virtue still. So Paul: “Shall we continue in sin so that grace may increase? Certainly not!#footnote[Rom 6:1, 2. The stone called Pantaurus has such power that it keeps the one who carries it from taking any harm from poison. The mercy of God in Christ to our souls is the most precious stone or pearl in the world to preserve us from being poisoned with sin.] How can we who died to sin live in it any longer?” There is nothing in the world that makes a man more unlike a saint, and more like Satan, than to argue from mercy to sinful liberty, from divine goodness to licentiousness. This is the devil’s logic, and in whomever you find it, you may write, “This soul is lost.” A man may as truly say that the sea burns or that fire cools, as that free grace and mercy should make a truly gracious soul do wickedly. So the same apostle: “Therefore I urge you#super[+], brothers, by the mercies of God, to offer your#super[+] bodies as living sacrifices, holy and pleasing to God, which is your#super[+] spiritual service of worship.” So John: “I am writing these things to you#super[+] so that you#super[+] will not sin.” What was it that he wrote? He wrote that we might have “fellowship … with the Father and with His Son,”#footnote[1 John 2:1, 2.] and that “the blood of Jesus His Son cleanses us from all sin,” and that “if we confess our sins, He is faithful and just to forgive us our sins,” and that if we do sin, “we have an advocate before the Father—Jesus Christ, the Righteous One.” These choice favors and mercies the apostle holds out as the choicest means to preserve the soul from sin and to keep it at the greatest distance from sin; and if this will not do it, you may write the man down as void of Christ and grace, and undone forever.
